\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces \textbf  {The substrate: a floor that is computed, the hypothesis it requires, and the cut key against ground truth.} \textbf  {(A)}~Floor computed---not assumed---as the minimum edge weight over four finite contact graphs, including an adversarial instance whose smallest weight is $10^{-12}$. Every instance returns a strictly positive floor, because positivity follows from finiteness together with strict positivity of the weights and not from their scale. \textbf  {(B)}~The failure branch. A system permitted to refine without limit generates thicknesses $1/n$ (orange): each is strictly positive and the infimum is zero, with every $\varepsilon $ down to $10^{-9}$ eventually cleared. Truncating the same sequence to a finite system (blue, first ten stages) restores a positive floor at $0.1$ (dashed). The uniform floor therefore requires finiteness, and the chart is included so that the requirement is visible rather than tacit. \textbf  {(C)}~The local separation cost of \cref  {eq:local-sepcost}, evaluated at a radius covering the graph, against an independent exhaustive enumeration of every bipartition, on $160$ graphs. Points lie on the identity to machine precision. This checks the implementation against ground truth rather than restating the definition. \textbf  {(D)}~Floor surface over edge count and weight scale. The surface is positive everywhere on the domain and descends toward zero only as the scale does; increasing the edge count at fixed scale lowers the floor without abolishing it, which is the content of a minimum over a finite set.}}{6}{figure.1}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:panel1}{{1}{6}{\textbf {The substrate: a floor that is computed, the hypothesis it requires, and the cut key against ground truth.} \textbf {(A)}~Floor computed---not assumed---as the minimum edge weight over four finite contact graphs, including an adversarial instance whose smallest weight is $10^{-12}$. Every instance returns a strictly positive floor, because positivity follows from finiteness together with strict positivity of the weights and not from their scale. \textbf {(B)}~The failure branch. A system permitted to refine without limit generates thicknesses $1/n$ (orange): each is strictly positive and the infimum is zero, with every $\varepsilon $ down to $10^{-9}$ eventually cleared. Truncating the same sequence to a finite system (blue, first ten stages) restores a positive floor at $0.1$ (dashed). The uniform floor therefore requires finiteness, and the chart is included so that the requirement is visible rather than tacit. \textbf {(C)}~The local separation cost of \cref {eq:local-sepcost}, evaluated at a radius covering the graph, against an independent exhaustive enumeration of every bipartition, on $160$ graphs. Points lie on the identity to machine precision. This checks the implementation against ground truth rather than restating the definition. \textbf {(D)}~Floor surface over edge count and weight scale. The surface is positive everywhere on the domain and descends toward zero only as the scale does; increasing the edge count at fixed scale lowers the floor without abolishing it, which is the content of a minimum over a finite set}{figure.1}{}}
