\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3}{\ignorespaces \textbf  {Intensivity requires a local normaliser, and the near-miss control is what shows it.} \textbf  {(A)}~Maximum drift in derived power when the graph is extended far from the item measured, over $120$ graphs, on a logarithmic axis. The intensive candidate does not move at all---the bar is at the floor of the axis because the value is exactly zero, not merely small. Both controls move by more than $10^{-1}$. \textbf  {(B)}~The control, and the reason panel~A carries information. Fraction of graphs on which each candidate moved: $0/120$ for the intensive candidate and $120/120$ for both controls. Had no control moved, the test would have been measuring something other than intensivity and would be reported as non-discriminating. \textbf  {(C)}~Power before against power after extension, for all three candidates. The intensive points lie on the identity exactly; the near-miss lies visibly above it, because the global floor fell and every power rose with it; the extensive points scatter below. The near-miss differs from the candidate in one component only---the normaliser---so its displacement localises the failure rather than merely exhibiting one. \textbf  {(D)}~Near-miss drift over the weight of the added edge and the number of items added. Drift is governed by the added \emph  {weight} and is nearly flat in the \emph  {count}, which is the signature of the global floor: one small distant edge is enough, and further additions change little.}}{10}{figure.3}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:panel3}{{3}{10}{\textbf {Intensivity requires a local normaliser, and the near-miss control is what shows it.} \textbf {(A)}~Maximum drift in derived power when the graph is extended far from the item measured, over $120$ graphs, on a logarithmic axis. The intensive candidate does not move at all---the bar is at the floor of the axis because the value is exactly zero, not merely small. Both controls move by more than $10^{-1}$. \textbf {(B)}~The control, and the reason panel~A carries information. Fraction of graphs on which each candidate moved: $0/120$ for the intensive candidate and $120/120$ for both controls. Had no control moved, the test would have been measuring something other than intensivity and would be reported as non-discriminating. \textbf {(C)}~Power before against power after extension, for all three candidates. The intensive points lie on the identity exactly; the near-miss lies visibly above it, because the global floor fell and every power rose with it; the extensive points scatter below. The near-miss differs from the candidate in one component only---the normaliser---so its displacement localises the failure rather than merely exhibiting one. \textbf {(D)}~Near-miss drift over the weight of the added edge and the number of items added. Drift is governed by the added \emph {weight} and is nearly flat in the \emph {count}, which is the signature of the global floor: one small distant edge is enough, and further additions change little}{figure.3}{}}
\newlabel{fig:panel3@cref}{{[figure][3][]3}{[1][9][]10}{}{}{}}
