\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4}{\ignorespaces \textbf  {Order dependence under sequential derivation is graded, not abolished.} \textbf  {(A)}~Distinct composite powers over all $720$ orderings of a six-item chain at radius $1$, on a logarithmic axis, with the line at $1$ marking exact order independence. The counts are $38$, $80$ and $432$; intensivity reduces dependence by an order of magnitude relative to the extensive control and no candidate attains $1$. The dashed line is drawn because the claim we set out to prove was that the intensive bar would sit on it. \textbf  {(B)}~The same counts at radius $0$ and radius $1$. Finer locality tightens the intensive count from $38$ to $11$ without reaching $1$: dependence survives because commitment mutates edges inside the balls of neighbouring items, which is exactly the case the intensivity theorem does not cover. \textbf  {(C)}~The distributions behind the counts: composite power over all $720$ orderings for the intensive candidate and the extensive control. The intensive distribution is narrow and the extensive one broad, and neither is a single spike. \textbf  {(D)}~Composite power over sampled orderings and radius for the intensive candidate. The surface is corrugated along the ordering axis at every radius---that corrugation is the residual order dependence---and its amplitude falls as the radius falls.}}{12}{figure.4}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:panel4}{{4}{12}{\textbf {Order dependence under sequential derivation is graded, not abolished.} \textbf {(A)}~Distinct composite powers over all $720$ orderings of a six-item chain at radius $1$, on a logarithmic axis, with the line at $1$ marking exact order independence. The counts are $38$, $80$ and $432$; intensivity reduces dependence by an order of magnitude relative to the extensive control and no candidate attains $1$. The dashed line is drawn because the claim we set out to prove was that the intensive bar would sit on it. \textbf {(B)}~The same counts at radius $0$ and radius $1$. Finer locality tightens the intensive count from $38$ to $11$ without reaching $1$: dependence survives because commitment mutates edges inside the balls of neighbouring items, which is exactly the case the intensivity theorem does not cover. \textbf {(C)}~The distributions behind the counts: composite power over all $720$ orderings for the intensive candidate and the extensive control. The intensive distribution is narrow and the extensive one broad, and neither is a single spike. \textbf {(D)}~Composite power over sampled orderings and radius for the intensive candidate. The surface is corrugated along the ordering axis at every radius---that corrugation is the residual order dependence---and its amplitude falls as the radius falls}{figure.4}{}}
\newlabel{fig:panel4@cref}{{[figure][4][]4}{[1][11][]12}{}{}{}}
