\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5}{\ignorespaces \textbf  {Composition is multiplicative, and control lies at the strongest rung.} \textbf  {(A)}~Mean absolute error of four candidate composition laws against a rung-by-rung simulation, over $4000$ ladders, on a logarithmic axis. The multiplicative law is exact to machine precision; the additive, maximum and mean laws err by $5.9\times 10^{-2}$, $2.1\times 10^{-1}$ and $4.6\times 10^{-1}$. All four are scored: scoring only the favoured law would not have distinguished it from the others. \textbf  {(B)}~Sensitivity normalised by the ladder's residual fraction, against rung power, over $700$ random ladders. Every point lies on $1/(1-\pi _j)$, which is exact, since sensitivity is $P/(1-\pi _j)$. The normalisation is necessary because raw sensitivity also depends on the other rungs, which obscures the relationship across ladders of differing composition. \textbf  {(C)}~Gain in composite power from a fixed improvement applied to each rung in turn, at three improvement sizes, for the four-rung ladder $(0.45,0.30,0.55,0.20)$. Rung~3, of highest power, returns most at every size and rung~4, the weakest, returns least. Improving the bottleneck is the worse investment, by roughly a factor of two at $\delta =0.1$. \textbf  {(D)}~Sensitivity to the third rung of a three-rung ladder, over the powers of the other two. The surface is $(1-\pi _1)(1-\pi _2)$: what a marginal gain buys is set by transmission through the remaining rungs, which is why the quantity being maximised is transmission and not slack.}}{15}{figure.5}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:panel5}{{5}{15}{\textbf {Composition is multiplicative, and control lies at the strongest rung.} \textbf {(A)}~Mean absolute error of four candidate composition laws against a rung-by-rung simulation, over $4000$ ladders, on a logarithmic axis. The multiplicative law is exact to machine precision; the additive, maximum and mean laws err by $5.9\times 10^{-2}$, $2.1\times 10^{-1}$ and $4.6\times 10^{-1}$. All four are scored: scoring only the favoured law would not have distinguished it from the others. \textbf {(B)}~Sensitivity normalised by the ladder's residual fraction, against rung power, over $700$ random ladders. Every point lies on $1/(1-\pi _j)$, which is exact, since sensitivity is $P/(1-\pi _j)$. The normalisation is necessary because raw sensitivity also depends on the other rungs, which obscures the relationship across ladders of differing composition. \textbf {(C)}~Gain in composite power from a fixed improvement applied to each rung in turn, at three improvement sizes, for the four-rung ladder $(0.45,0.30,0.55,0.20)$. Rung~3, of highest power, returns most at every size and rung~4, the weakest, returns least. Improving the bottleneck is the worse investment, by roughly a factor of two at $\delta =0.1$. \textbf {(D)}~Sensitivity to the third rung of a three-rung ladder, over the powers of the other two. The surface is $(1-\pi _1)(1-\pi _2)$: what a marginal gain buys is set by transmission through the remaining rungs, which is why the quantity being maximised is transmission and not slack}{figure.5}{}}
