\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {6}{\ignorespaces \textbf  {The clock, the refusal, and label independence with its control.} \textbf  {(A)}~The clock $M$ under the two regimes. Free rules---forming a rung, deriving a rung, reading a composite---leave $M$ flat at zero however many are performed; a climb advances it once per rung. This is the operational content of the freeness criterion, which licenses a free rule exactly when it commits no cut. \textbf  {(B)}~The static verdict against execution on $5000$ generated programs: $4032$ accepted, $968$ refused, and zero disagreements, marked by the cross at zero. Both verdicts occur in quantity, so the test could have failed in either direction; a suite in which every program was accepted would not have tested rejection. \textbf  {(C)}~Label independence and the control that gives it content. The vertical line at zero is the distribution of changes under relabelling: every one is exactly zero, since a rung has no identity field and the relabelling acts on annotations external to the formalism. The histogram is the control, in which a rung \emph  {power} is perturbed instead; those changes are entirely positive and well away from zero. Without the control the first result would be consistent with a constant observable and would carry no information. \textbf  {(D)}~Rungs required to reach a target, over target and rung power. The surface diverges as the target approaches unity and as the rung power approaches zero, and is low only where strong rungs meet modest targets. This is the cost model behind the refusal: what a target costs is set jointly by how demanding it is and by how much each step accomplishes.}}{17}{figure.6}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:panel6}{{6}{17}{\textbf {The clock, the refusal, and label independence with its control.} \textbf {(A)}~The clock $M$ under the two regimes. Free rules---forming a rung, deriving a rung, reading a composite---leave $M$ flat at zero however many are performed; a climb advances it once per rung. This is the operational content of the freeness criterion, which licenses a free rule exactly when it commits no cut. \textbf {(B)}~The static verdict against execution on $5000$ generated programs: $4032$ accepted, $968$ refused, and zero disagreements, marked by the cross at zero. Both verdicts occur in quantity, so the test could have failed in either direction; a suite in which every program was accepted would not have tested rejection. \textbf {(C)}~Label independence and the control that gives it content. The vertical line at zero is the distribution of changes under relabelling: every one is exactly zero, since a rung has no identity field and the relabelling acts on annotations external to the formalism. The histogram is the control, in which a rung \emph {power} is perturbed instead; those changes are entirely positive and well away from zero. Without the control the first result would be consistent with a constant observable and would carry no information. \textbf {(D)}~Rungs required to reach a target, over target and rung power. The surface diverges as the target approaches unity and as the rung power approaches zero, and is low only where strong rungs meet modest targets. This is the cost model behind the refusal: what a target costs is set jointly by how demanding it is and by how much each step accomplishes}{figure.6}{}}
\newlabel{fig:panel6@cref}{{[figure][6][]6}{[1][17][]17}{}{}{}}
